# R42 tail-reserve boundary cell.
#
# break-only-if-it-NARROWS · the SEPARATOR cell. The suppression is restricted
# to `i == 0 && i == last` — the ONE-ITEM list — and this fixture is why.
#
# At `i > 0` the `>=` corner IS reachable: a one-character open delimiter plus
# a one-character first item lands the second item EXACTLY on the continuation
# column, so a rule spelled `i == 0` alone (or with no index test at all) fires
# there too. The natural minimal implementation of "suppress the newline"
# at that position silently DROPS the `, ` separator, producing output that
# still re-parses — as a DIFFERENT program — and no width assertion can see it.
#
# The restriction kills the hazard structurally and loses nothing: the one-item
# shape was always the motivating cell.
#
# There is no pre-fix RED — the shipped rule cannot reach this corner. Its red
# is a break-and-watch: widen the suppression predicate to `i == 0` (or drop
# the index test) and rebuild; the `, ` between `1` and the string disappears.
#
# The overrun below is the DOCTRINAL unbreakable-atom escape.

void main():
    print([1, "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa"], 2)
